                        </origin>
                        <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_857">Harlaston,
                                Staffordshire. Parish church</orgName>
                            <p>In calendar (February 9) <q><persName role="dnr" key="person_3971"
                                        >Iohannes Vernon</persName> eques auratus, obiit ... anno a
                                    Christo nato 1545 ... qui(?) Iohannes ecclesie in harlastoune
                                    istum librum donavit</q>. Other entries in the calendar mostly
                                refer to de Veres (Earls of Oxford) and Saunfords (of Sanford,
                                Oxfordshire?), also obits of <persName>Sir Richard Vernon of
                                    Haddon</persName> (d. 1450), his son <persName>William
                                    Vernon</persName> (d. 1467), his son <persName>Henry
                                    Vernon</persName> (d. 1515), his daughter-in-law
                                    <persName>Anne</persName> (d. 1493), their son <persName>Sir
                                    John Vernon</persName> of Sudbury, the donor of the book (d.
                                1545), his son <persName>Sir Henry Vernon of Sudbury</persName> (d.
                                1569). Harlaston belonged to the Vernons; see <bibl>Shaw, <title>The
                                        History and Antiquities of Staffordshire ... </title>
                                    (1798), I, p. 87</bibl>.</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_LaterOwners"><q>Mr Walter Grey his book</q> (s.
                            xvii)</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_LaterOwners">Given to Christs by Simon Every, rector
                            of Navenby, Lincolnshire in 1718.</provenance>
                    </history>
